Ladybird keeps extensions out of private windows unless a saved
allowed_in_private setting, false by default, lets them in.
The registry filters the descriptors each private process gets, and
the UI process rejects private context and resource requests without the
opt-in. One background page, in the normal profile, serves both kinds of
windows, with one storage.

Chromium saves the opt-in as the kPrefIncognitoEnabled preference, which
ExtensionPrefs::IsIncognitoEnabled() reads and SetIsIncognitoEnabled()
sets. Gecko checks PRIVATE_ALLOWED_PERMISSION in its Extension class’s
_setupStartupPermissions(), and saves it through ExtensionPermissions.
WebKit updates m_hasAccessToPrivateData in WebExtensionContext::
setHasAccessToPrivateData(), which also adds or removes injected
content in private pages. Ladybird matches that opt-in gate
in all three. Its single shared background matches Chromium’s default
“spanning” incognito mode.
